research/wave7-dispatch/orchestrator-A3-judgment.log-19062-**False claim:** there is a sequence $(b_k)$ of reals with $b_k > 0$ for every $k \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $\sum b_k$ diverges ([[def-series]]) and such that **every** sequence $(a_k)$ of reals with $a_k > 0$ for every $k$ and $\sum a_k$ divergent satisfies $$b_k \le a_k \quad \text{for all } k \text{ from some index on.}$$ Such a $(b_k)$ would be a *slowest* divergent series of positive terms, and it would give a universal comparison test: a positive series would diverge exactly when its terms eventually dominate those of $(b_k)$. **No such sequence exists.** The refutation is direct and uses no choice: given any divergent $\sum b_k$ with p
