research/wave7-dispatch/orchestrator-A3-judgment.log:19065:ex-harmonic-series-diverges	example	"The harmonic series $\\sum 1/k$ diverges, by condensation and by Oresme block grouping"
research/wave7-dispatch/orchestrator-A3-judgment.log-19066-The **harmonic series** is $\sum_{k \ge 1} 1/k$, the series from the starting index $1$ ([[def-series]]) of the family $a_k = 1/\iota(k)$; the index $0$ is excluded because $1/0$ has no value. It **diverges**, and its partial sums are unbounded above. This is the case $p = 1$ of [[thm-p-series-rational]]. The two arguments below do not use that theorem: the first is the condensation argument, which is how [[thm-p-series-rational]] itself is proved and which here degenerates to something one can read off; the second is Oresme's block grouping from the fourteenth century, which uses no test at all and produces the explicit lower bound $$\sum_{k
