research/wave7-dispatch/orchestrator-A3-judgment.log-19090-Take $a_k := 1/\iota(k+1)^{2}$ for $k \in \mathbb{N}$, so that $\sum a_k$ is $\sum_{k \ge 1} 1/k^{2}$ ([[def-series]]). Then: - its ratios $q_k = a_{k+1}/a_k = \dfrac{(k+1)^{2}}{(k+2)^{2}}$ converge to $1$, so **neither half of the ratio test applies** ([[thm-ratio-test]]); - its Raabe expression is exactly $$R_k \;=\; (k+1)\left(\frac{a_k}{a_{k+1}} - 1\right) \;=\; \frac{(k+2)^{2} - (k+1)^{2}}{k+1} \;=\; \frac{2k+3}{k+1} \;=\; 2 + \frac{1}{k+1} ,$$ which exceeds $2$ at every index, so $\liminf_k R_k \ge 2 > 1$ and **Raabe's test gives convergence** ([[cor-raabe-test]]). This is the smallest honest illustration that Raabe's test decides serie
