research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series-a4-recovery.log-5335-- **Why the two arguments are the same argument.** Oresme's blocks are the blocks of the condensation proof, grouped from $2^{n}+1$ to $2^{n+1}$, and the constant $1/2$ in step 1.2 is the constant that makes the condensed terms of step 3.1 equal to $1$. The difference is bookkeeping: condensation states the grouping once and for all, for every nonincreasing family, and the block argument performs it for this one family.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series-a4-recovery.log-5336-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series-a4-recovery.log-5337-- **The divergence is extremely slow, and the bound says how slow.** To make the partial sum exceed $M$ the estimate of step 2.2 asks for about $2^{2M}$ terms. That is why the harmonic series is the standard warning against reading convergence off numerical evidence.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series-a4-recovery.log-5338-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series-a4-recovery.log-5339-- **The bound in step 2.2 is one sided.** Nothing here says the partial sums are *at most* $1 + n/2$ along powers of $2$, and in fact they are not; the matching upper bound is the other half of the condensation estimate, and it is not needed for divergence.
