research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7087-          "quote": "Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b \\in F$.\n\n1. If $a > 0$ then $a^{-1} > 0$.\n2. If $0 < a < b$ then $0 < b^{-1} < a^{-1}$.",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7088-          "uses": [
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7089-            "1.1",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7090-            "1.3"
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7091-          ]
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7092-        },
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7093-        {
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7094-          "fact": "L2",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json:7095:          "source": "ex-harmonic-series-diverges",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7096-          "source_section": "Example",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json:7097:          "quote": "The **harmonic series** is $\\sum_{k \\ge 1} 1/k$, the series from the starting\nindex $1$ ([[def-series]]) of the family $a_k = 1/\\iota(k)$; the index $0$ is\nexcluded because $1/0$ has no value. It **diverges**, and its partial sums are\nunbounded above.\n\nThis is the case $p = 1$ of [[thm-p-series-rational]]. The two arguments below do\nnot use that theorem: the first is the condensation argument, which is how\n[[thm-p-series-rational]] itself is proved and which here degenerates to\nsomething one can read off; the second is Oresme's block grouping from the\nfourteenth century, which uses no test at all and produces the explicit lower\nbound\n\n$$\\sum_{k=1}^{2^{n}} \\frac{1}{k} \\;\\ge\\; 1 + \\frac{n}{2} \\qquad (n \\in \\mathbb{N}) .$$\n\nThat bound is worth having on its own: it says the harmonic partial sums grow at\nleast like a constant multiple of $n$ along the powers of $2$, which is the\nslowest divergence any explicit series on this page exhibits.",
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7098-          "uses": [
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7099-            "1.2"
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7100-          ]
research/audit/wave7-real-analysis-series.proof-contracts.json-7101-        },
