items/thm-of-archimedean.md:34:## Statement
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-35-
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-36-Every complete ordered field $F$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) is Archimedean:
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-37-for every $x \in F$ there is a natural number $n \ge 1$ with $x < n \cdot 1_F$,
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-38-where $n \cdot 1_F$ is the canonical natural of the ordered field $F$
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-39-([[def-ordered-field]]). Equivalently, the canonical naturals are cofinal in $F$.
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-40-
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:41:## Facts & Assumptions
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-42-
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-43-**Given:** A complete ordered field $F$; write $A = \{\, n \cdot 1_F : n \ge 1 \,\}$ for the set of its canonical naturals.
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-44-
--
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-48-
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-49-[L3] $0 < 1_F$ ([[cor-of-one-positive]]).
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-50-
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:51:## Proof
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-52-
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-53-**Proof technique:** contradiction.
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-54-
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:55:1.1 Suppose, for contradiction, that $F$ is not Archimedean: there is some $x \in F$ with $n \cdot 1_F \le x$ for all $n \ge 1$, that is, $x$ is an upper bound of $A$. [assume-contra]
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-56-
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:57:2.1 The set $A$ is nonempty, since $1 \cdot 1_F = 1_F \in A$, and it is bounded above by $x$. [step 1.1, L2]
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-58-
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:59:3.1 By the least-upper-bound property, $A$ has a least upper bound $s = \sup A \in F$. [step 2.1, L1]
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-60-
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:61:4.1 Since $1_F > 0$, we have $s - 1_F < s$; as $s$ is the least upper bound, $s - 1_F$ is not an upper bound of $A$. [step 3.1, L3]
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-62-
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:63:5.1 Hence there is some $m \ge 1$ with $m \cdot 1_F > s - 1_F$. [step 4.1]
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-64-
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:65:6.1 Adding $1_F$ to both sides, $(m+1) \cdot 1_F = m \cdot 1_F + 1_F > s$. [step 5.1, L2]
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-66-
items/thm-of-archimedean.md-67-7.1 But $(m+1) \cdot 1_F \in A$, so $(m+1) \cdot 1_F \le s$ because $s$ is an upper bound of $A$, contradicting 6.1. [step 6.1, step 3.1, L2]
