items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-33-
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md:34:## Statement
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-35-
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-36-Let $F$ be an ordered field, with field structure as in [[def-field]], and for
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-37-$n \ge 1$ let $n \cdot 1_F$ denote the canonical natural number
--
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-42-- the map $n \mapsto n \cdot 1_F$ is strictly increasing, hence injective, on $\{1, 2, 3, \dots\}$;
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-43-- $(m+n) \cdot 1_F = m \cdot 1_F + n \cdot 1_F$ and $(mn) \cdot 1_F = (m \cdot 1_F)(n \cdot 1_F)$ for all $m, n \ge 1$.
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-44-
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md:45:## Facts & Assumptions
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-46-
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-47-**Given:** An ordered field $F$ whose canonical naturals are defined recursively by $1 \cdot 1_F = 1_F$ and $(n+1) \cdot 1_F = n \cdot 1_F + 1_F$, with $0 \cdot 1_F = 0$.
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-48-
--
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-50-
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-51-[L2] The order is compatible with addition and positives are closed under addition: $a < b$ implies $a + c < b + c$, and $a > 0$, $b > 0$ imply $a + b > 0$ ([[lem-of-add-order]]).
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-52-
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md:53:## Proof
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-54-
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-55-**Proof technique:** induction.
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-56-
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md:57:1.1 Base case $n = 1$: by definition $1 \cdot 1_F = 1_F$, and $1_F > 0$. [L1, base]
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-58-
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md:59:1.2 Inductive hypothesis: assume $n \cdot 1_F > 0$ for some fixed $n \ge 1$. [ih]
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-60-
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md:61:1.3 By the recursion, $(n+1) \cdot 1_F - n \cdot 1_F = 1_F > 0$, so $(n+1) \cdot 1_F > n \cdot 1_F$. [given, L1]
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-62-
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md:63:1.4 Additive identity: fix $m$ and induct on $n$; at $n = 1$, $(m+1) \cdot 1_F = m \cdot 1_F + 1_F$ by the recursion, and if $(m+n) \cdot 1_F = m \cdot 1_F + n \cdot 1_F$ then $(m+n+1) \cdot 1_F = (m+n) \cdot 1_F + 1_F = m \cdot 1_F + n \cdot 1_F + 1_F = m \cdot 1_F + (n+1) \cdot 1_F$. [given, algebra]
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-64-
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md:65:2.1 Inductive step: $(n+1) \cdot 1_F = n \cdot 1_F + 1_F$ is a sum of two positives, by the hypothesis and $1_F > 0$, hence $(n+1) \cdot 1_F > 0$. [step 1.2, L1, L2]
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-66-
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md:67:2.2 Iterating 1.3, for $m < n$ one has $m \cdot 1_F < n \cdot 1_F$, so $n \mapsto n \cdot 1_F$ is strictly increasing and therefore injective on $\{1, 2, 3, \dots\}$. [step 1.3, L2]
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-68-
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md:69:2.3 Multiplicative identity: fix $m$ and induct on $n$; at $n = 1$, $(m \cdot 1) \cdot 1_F = m \cdot 1_F = (m \cdot 1_F)(1 \cdot 1_F)$, and the step $(m(n+1)) \cdot 1_F = (mn + m) \cdot 1_F = (mn) \cdot 1_F + m \cdot 1_F = (m \cdot 1_F)(n \cdot 1_F) + (m \cdot 1_F)(1_F) = (m \cdot 1_F)((n+1) \cdot 1_F)$ uses 1.4 and distributivity. [step 1.4, algebra]
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md-70-
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md:71:3.1 By induction on 1.1, 1.2, 2.1, $n \cdot 1_F > 0$ for every $n \ge 1$; with the strict increase 2.2 and the identities 1.4, 2.3, every claim holds. [step 2.1, step 2.2, step 1.4, step 2.3, discharge-induction] ∎
--
