items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md-32-
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md:33:## Statement
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md-34-
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md-35-Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md-36-let $a, b \in F$.
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md-37-
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md:38:1. If $a > 0$ then $a^{-1} > 0$.
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md:39:2. If $0 < a < b$ then $0 < b^{-1} < a^{-1}$.
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md-40-
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md:41:## Facts & Assumptions
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md-42-
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md-43-**Given:** An ordered field $F$ with positive cone $P$, and elements $a, b \in F$.
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md-44-
--
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md-50-
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md-51-[L4] $P$ is closed under addition, so $<$ is transitive ([[def-ordered-field]]).
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md-52-
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md:53:## Proof
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md-54-
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md-55-**Proof technique:** direct.
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md-56-
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md:57:1.1 Assume $a > 0$, so $a \neq 0$ and its inverse $a^{-1}$ exists with $a \cdot a^{-1} = 1$; moreover $a^{-1} \neq 0$, since $a^{-1}$ has $a$ as its inverse while $0$ is non-invertible ($1 \neq 0$ by L3). [assume-hyp, L1, L3]
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md-58-
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md:59:2.1 By trichotomy $a^{-1} \in P$ or $-a^{-1} \in P$; if $-a^{-1} \in P$, then $a > 0$ and $a^{-1} < 0$ give $a \cdot a^{-1} < 0$ by the sign rules, i.e. $1 < 0$, contradicting $0 < 1$; hence $a^{-1} \in P$, i.e. $a^{-1} > 0$, proving claim 1. [step 1.1, L2, L3, L1]
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md-60-
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md:61:3.1 Assume $0 < a < b$; then $0 < b$ by transitivity, so by claim 1 both $a^{-1} > 0$ and $b^{-1} > 0$, and the sign rules give $a^{-1} b^{-1} > 0$. [assume-hyp, step 2.1, L4, L2, L1]
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md-62-
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md:63:4.1 Multiplying $a < b$ by the positive $a^{-1} b^{-1}$ via the sign rules gives $a (a^{-1} b^{-1}) < b (a^{-1} b^{-1})$; since $a a^{-1} = 1$ and $b b^{-1} = 1$, this simplifies to $b^{-1} < a^{-1}$. [step 3.1, L2, algebra]
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md-64-
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md:65:5.1 Together with $b^{-1} > 0$ from step 3.1, we conclude $0 < b^{-1} < a^{-1}$, proving claim 2. [step 3.1, step 4.1] ∎
--
