items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-40-Then:
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-41-
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md:42:1. $(s_n)$ is nondecreasing ([[def-monotone-sequence]]) and $s_n \ge 0$ for every
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-43-   $n$;
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md:44:2. $\sum a_k$ converges **if and only if** $S$ is bounded above
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-45-   ([[def-bounded-set]]), and in that case
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-46-   $$\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} a_k \;=\; \sup S ,$$
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-47-   so in particular $s_n \le \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} a_k$ for every $n$;
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md:48:3. if $S$ is not bounded above then $s_n \to +\infty$
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-49-   ([[def-divergence-to-infinity]]) and $\sum a_k$ diverges.
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-50-
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-51-This is the theorem that makes the nonnegative theory work: for terms of one
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-52-sign, convergence is a boundedness question and no candidate limit is ever
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-53-needed. Every comparison test on this page is an application of it.
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-54-
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md:55:## Facts & Assumptions
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-56-
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-57-**Given:** A sequence $(a_k)$ of reals with $a_k \ge 0$ for every $k$, its partial sums $s_n = \sum_{k<n} a_k$, and the range $S = \{s_n : n \in \mathbb{N}\}$ ([[def-series]], [[def-finite-sum]], [[def-sequence]]).
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-58-
--
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-68-
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-69-[L6] A nondecreasing sequence whose range is not bounded above diverges to $+\infty$ ([[lem-monotone-unbounded-diverges]], [[def-divergence-to-infinity]]).
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-70-
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md:71:## Proof
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-72-
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-73-**Proof technique:** direct.
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-74-
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md:75:1.1 For every $n$, $s_{n+1} - s_n = a_n \ge 0$, so $s_n \le s_{n+1}$ and $(s_n)$ is nondecreasing. [given, L1, L2]
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-76-
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md:77:1.2 For every $n$, $s_n = \sum_{k<n} a_k \ge 0$, all terms being nonnegative. [given, L3]
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-78-
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md:79:2.1 Claim 1 is steps 1.1 and 1.2 together. [step 1.1, step 1.2]
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-80-
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md:81:2.2 Since $s_n \ge 0$ we have $|s_n| = s_n$, so $(s_n)$ is bounded in the sense of [L4] if and only if $S$ is bounded above. [step 1.2, L4]
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-82-
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md:83:3.1 By [L4] applied to the monotone sequence $(s_n)$, the series converges if and only if $(s_n)$ is bounded, hence if and only if $S$ is bounded above. [step 1.1, step 2.2, L4]
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-84-
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md:85:4.1 If $S$ is bounded above then $(s_n)$ converges to $\sup S$, so $\sum a_k$ converges with sum $\sup S$; and since $\sup S$ is an upper bound of $S$, $s_n \le \sup S$ for every $n$. [step 1.1, step 3.1, L5]
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-86-
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md:87:4.2 If $S$ is not bounded above then $s_n \to +\infty$, and by step 3.1 the series diverges. [step 1.1, step 3.1, L6]
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-88-
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md:89:5.1 The equivalence and the identification of the sum as the supremum together make claim 2, and the divergence statement is claim 3. [step 3.1, step 4.1, step 4.2] ∎
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-90-
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md:91:## Remarks
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-92-
items/thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums.md-93-- **"Bounded" and "bounded above" coincide here, and only here.** The equivalence used in step 2.2 rests on $s_n \ge 0$, which rests on every term being nonnegative. For a series with terms of both signs the partial sums can be bounded above and still fail to converge, so nothing in this theorem survives the loss of the sign hypothesis. That failure is exhibited by [[cex-comparison-needs-nonnegativity]] on the companion page.
