items/lem-finite-sum-laws.md-42-
items/lem-finite-sum-laws.md:43:## Statement
items/lem-finite-sum-laws.md-44-
items/lem-finite-sum-laws.md-45-Let $a, b, c : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$ be sequences of reals, let
items/lem-finite-sum-laws.md-46-$\lambda \in \mathbb{R}$, and let $m, n \in \mathbb{N}$, with finite sums and
items/lem-finite-sum-laws.md-47-finite products as in [[def-finite-sum]]. Then:
items/lem-finite-sum-laws.md-48-
items/lem-finite-sum-laws.md:49:1. **Additivity.** $\displaystyle\sum_{k<n}(a_k + b_k) = \sum_{k<n} a_k + \sum_{k<n} b_k$.
items/lem-finite-sum-laws.md:50:2. **Scaling.** $\displaystyle\sum_{k<n} \lambda a_k = \lambda \sum_{k<n} a_k$; in particular $\displaystyle\sum_{k<n} \lambda = n\lambda$, where $n$ denotes the canonical natural $\iota(n) \in \mathbb{R}$ ([[lem-of-q-embeds]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]).
items/lem-finite-sum-laws.md:51:3. **Splitting.** If $m \le n$ then $\displaystyle\sum_{k<n} a_k = \sum_{k<m} a_k + \sum_{k=m}^{n-1} a_k$, and $\displaystyle\prod_{k<n} a_k = \Big(\prod_{k<m} a_k\Big)\Big(\prod_{k=m}^{n-1} a_k\Big)$.
items/lem-finite-sum-laws.md:52:4. **Monotonicity.** If $a_k \le b_k$ for all $k < n$ then $\displaystyle\sum_{k<n} a_k \le \sum_{k<n} b_k$. In particular, if $a_k \ge 0$ for all $k < n$ then $\sum_{k<n} a_k \ge 0$, every single term satisfies $a_j \le \sum_{k<n} a_k$ for $j < n$, and $\sum_{k<n} a_k = 0$ forces $a_k = 0$ for every $k < n$.
items/lem-finite-sum-laws.md:53:5. **Telescoping.** $\displaystyle\sum_{k<n}\big(c_{k+1} - c_k\big) = c_n - c_0$.
items/lem-finite-sum-laws.md:54:6. **Products.** $\displaystyle\prod_{k<n}(a_k b_k) = \Big(\prod_{k<n} a_k\Big)\Big(\prod_{k<n} b_k\Big)$; if $a_k \ge 0$ for all $k < n$ then $\prod_{k<n} a_k \ge 0$, and if $a_k > 0$ for all $k < n$ then $\prod_{k<n} a_k > 0$.
items/lem-finite-sum-laws.md-55-
items/lem-finite-sum-laws.md:56:## Facts & Assumptions
items/lem-finite-sum-laws.md-57-
items/lem-finite-sum-laws.md-58-**Given:** Sequences $a, b, c : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$, a real $\lambda$, and naturals $m, n$. Write $\Sigma_n = \sum_{k<n} a_k$ and $\Pi_n = \prod_{k<n} a_k$.
